The volume of a right square‑based pyramid is  

\[
V=\frac13(\text{base area})\times(\text{height})=\frac13 s^{2}h .
\]

Given \(V=63{,}960\text{ m}^3\) and \(h=30\text{ m}\),

\[
\frac13 s^{2}\cdot30 = 63{,}960\quad\Longrightarrow\quad s^{2}= \frac{3\cdot63{,}960}{30}=6{,}396 .
\]

Hence  

\[
s=\sqrt{6{,}396}\approx 79.975\text{ m}.
\]

The distance from the centre of the base to the midpoint of a side is half the side length:

\[
\frac{s}{2}\approx\frac{79.975}{2}=39.9875\text{ m}.
\]

The slant height (the lateral height \(\overline{AB}\)) is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are the pyramid’s height \(h=30\) m and \(\frac{s}{2}\):

\[
AB=\sqrt{h^{2}+\left(\frac{s}{2}\right)^{2}}
      =\sqrt{30^{2}+1599}
      =\sqrt{2499}\approx 49.99\text{ m}.
\]

Rounded to the nearest whole number,  

\[
AB\approx 50\text{ m}.
\]

\[
\boxed{50}
\]<|im_end|>

results.solution:
Rotating $360^\circ$ is the same as doing nothing, so rotating $600^\circ$ is the same as rotating $600^\circ - 360^\circ = 240^\circ$.  Therefore, we have $\sin 600^\circ = \sin (600^\circ - 360^\circ) = \sin 240^\circ$.

Let $P$ be the point on the unit circle that is $240^\circ$ counterclockwise from $(1,0)$, and let $D$ be the foot of the altitude from $P$ to the $x$-axis, as shown below.

[asy]
pair A,C,P,O,D;
draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2),p=black+1.2bp,Arrows(0.15cm));
draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0),p=black+1.2bp,Arrows(0.15cm));
A = (1,0);
O= (0,0);
label("$x$",(1.2,0),SE);
label("$y$",(0,1.2),NE);

P = rotate(240)*A;
D = foot(P,A,-A);
draw(O--P--D);
draw(rightanglemark(O,D,P,2));
draw(Circle(O,1));
label("$O$",O,SE);
label("$P$",P,SW);
//label("$A$",A,SE);
label("$D$",D,N);
[/asy]

Triangle $POD$ is a 30-60-90 triangle, so $DO = \frac{1}{2}$ and $DP = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.  Therefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\left(-\frac12,-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)$, so $\sin 600^\circ = \sin240^\circ = \boxed{-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}}$.

