The large semicircle has radius \(2\), so its area is  

\[
\frac12\pi(2)^2 = 2\pi .
\]

Each small semicircle has radius \(1\); its area is  

\[
\frac12\pi(1)^2 = \frac{\pi}{2}.
\]

There are three such semicircles, so their total area is  

\[
3\cdot\frac{\pi}{2}= \frac{3\pi}{2}.
\]

The three small semicircles overlap.  
Two adjacent semicircles (e.g., those centered at \(-1\) and \(0\)) intersect.  
For two circles of radius \(1\) whose centers are distance \(1\) apart, the area of overlap of the full circles is  

\[
2r^{2}\cos^{-1}\!\left(\frac{d}{2r}\right)-\frac{d}{2}\sqrt{4r^{2}-d^{2}}
=2\cos^{-1}\!\left(\frac12\right)-\frac12\sqrt{3}
= \frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

Since each semicircle is only the upper half, the overlap of two adjacent semicircles is half of this:

\[
\frac12\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)=\frac{\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}.
\]

There are two such adjacent pairs, so the total area of pairwise overlaps is  

\[
2\left(\frac{\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\right)=\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

The three semicircles have no triple overlap (they meet only at a point), so by inclusion–exclusion the area of their union is  

\[
\frac{3\pi}{2}-\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)
= \frac{5\pi}{6}+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

Finally, the shaded region is the part of the large semicircle not covered by the small ones:

\[
\text{Shaded area}= 2\pi-\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)
= \frac{7\pi}{6}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{7\pi}{6}-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}}
\]<|im_end|>

