The two isosceles triangles give the equalities  

\[
BD = BC ,\qquad AD = AC .
\]

Hence \(D\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \(\overline{BC}\) and \(A\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \(\overline{CD}\).

Let  

\[
\angle ABC = 70^{\circ},\qquad \angle BAC = x,\qquad 
\angle ACB = 110^{\circ}-x .
\]

Because \(BD = BC\), triangle \(BDC\) is isosceles with vertex at \(B\); therefore  

\[
\angle DBC = \angle DCB .
\]

Since \(\angle ABC = 70^{\circ}\) and \(\angle ACB = 110^{\circ}-x\),

\[
\angle DBC = 70^{\circ},\qquad 
\angle DCB = 110^{\circ}-x .
\]

Thus the apex angle of \(\triangle BDC\) is  

\[
\angle DBC = 70^{\circ}.
\]

In an isosceles triangle the base angles are equal, so  

\[
\angle BDC = \angle DCB = \frac{180^{\circ}-70^{\circ}}{2}=55^{\circ}.
\]

Now look at \(\triangle ACD\).  
It is isosceles with \(AD = AC\); consequently the base angles at \(D\) and \(C\) are equal:

\[
\angle ADC = \angle ACD .
\]

But \(\angle ADC\) is the exterior angle at \(D\) of \(\triangle BDC\); hence  

\[
\angle ADC = 180^{\circ}-\angle BDC = 180^{\circ}-55^{\circ}=125^{\circ}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\angle ACD = 125^{\circ}.
\]

Since \(\angle ACD\) is the angle between \(CA\) and \(CD\), and \(\angle ACB\) is the angle between \(CA\) and \(CB\),

\[
\angle ACD = \angle ACB + \angle BCD .
\]

We already have \(\angle ACB = 110^{\circ}-x\) and \(\angle BCD = 55^{\circ}\); thus  

\[
125^{\circ}= (110^{\circ}-x)+55^{\circ}.
\]

Solving for \(x\),

\[
125^{\circ}=165^{\circ}-x\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x = 40^{\circ}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{40}
\] is the measure of \(\angle BAC\).<|im_end|>

