The prism has an equilateral triangular base of side \(12\) and height \(16\).

---

### 1.  Set up coordinates  

Place the top triangle \(ABC\) in the plane \(z=0\) and the bottom triangle \(DEF\) in the plane \(z=-16\).

\[
\begin{aligned}
A&=(0,0,0),\\
B&=(12,0,0),\\
C&=\Bigl(6,\;6\sqrt3,\;0\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

The three lateral edges are vertical, so the bottom vertices are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
E&=(0,0,-16),\\
F&=(12,0,-16),\\
D&=\Bigl(6,\;6\sqrt3,\;-16\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the height of the prism is the length of each lateral edge, \(16\).

---

### 2.  Locate the points \(M,N,P,Q\)

* \(M\) lies on \(DE\) with \(DM=4\).  
  Since \(|DE|=12\), \(M\) is \(\frac{4}{12}=\frac13\) of the way from \(D\) to \(E\):
  \[
  M = D+\frac13(E-D)=\Bigl(6,\;6\sqrt3,\;-12\Bigr).
  \]

* \(N\) lies on \(DF\) with \(DN=2\).  
  Hence \(N\) is \(\frac{2}{12}=\frac16\) of the way from \(D\) to \(F\):
  \[
  N = D+\frac16(F-D)=\Bigl(7,\;5\sqrt3,\;-15\Bigr).
  \]

* \(P\) lies on \(CB\) with \(CP=4\) (because \(CQ=8\) and the whole side is \(12\)).  
  So \(P\) is \(\frac{4}{12}=\frac13\) of the way from \(C\) to \(B\):
  \[
  P = C+\frac13(B-C)=\Bigl(10,\;2\sqrt3,\;0\Bigr).
  \]

* \(Q\) lies on \(CA\) with \(CQ=8\).  
  Hence \(Q\) is \(\frac{8}{12}=\frac23\) of the way from \(C\) to \(A\):
  \[
  Q = C+\frac23(A-C)=\Bigl(2,\;2\sqrt3,\;0\Bigr).
  \]

All four points are coplanar; indeed the plane through them also passes through the points \(C\) and \(D\).

---

### 3.  Describe the cut polyhedron  

The plane cuts the prism along the quadrilateral \(MNPQ\).  
The part of the prism that contains the edge \(CD\) is the polyhedron with vertices  

\[
Q,\;P,\;C,\;D,\;M,\;N .
\]

Thus the solid \(QPCDMN\) consists of the two triangular faces \(CPQ\) and \(DMN\) together with the lateral faces \(CMN,\;CDM,\;CDN,\;CPN,\;CQN\).

---

### 4.  Compute its volume  

Because the plane contains the line \(CD\), we can split the polyhedron into two tetrahedra that share the base triangle \(CDM\) (or any convenient base).  
A convenient decomposition is:

\[
\operatorname{Vol}(QPCDMN)=\operatorname{Vol}(C\,D\,M\,N)+\operatorname{Vol}(C\,D\,N\,P).
\]

Both tetrahedra have the same base triangle \(CDN\) (or \(CDM\)); we use the base \(CDN\).

* **Base triangle \(CDN\).**  
  Vectors:
  \[
  \vec{DC}= (0,0,16),\qquad 
  \vec{DN}= (1,\;-\sqrt3,\;1).
  \]
  Hence
  \[
  \text{Area}(CDN)=\frac12\bigl\lVert\vec{DC}\times\vec{DN}\bigr\rVert
  =\frac12\bigl\lVert(0,0,16)\times(1,-\sqrt3,1)\bigr\rVert
  =\frac12\cdot16\sqrt{1+3}=8\sqrt3 .
  \]

* **Height of tetrahedron \(C\,D\,N\,P\).**  
  The distance from \(P\) to the plane of triangle \(CDN\) is the perpendicular distance from \(P\) to the line \(CD\).  
  Using the formula for distance from a point to a line in 3‑D,
  \[
  \text{dist}(P,CD)=\frac{\bigl\lVert(\overrightarrow{CP}\times\overrightarrow{CD})\bigr\rVert}{\lVert\overrightarrow{CD}\rVert}
  =\frac{4\sqrt3}{4}= \sqrt3 .
  \]

  Therefore
  \[
  \operatorname{Vol}(C\,D\,N\,P)=\frac13\bigl(\text{Area}(CDN)\bigr)\times\text{height}
  =\frac13\,(8\sqrt3)\,(\sqrt3)=8 .
  \]

* **Height of tetrahedron \(C\,D\,M\,N\).**  
  By symmetry the same calculation gives the same height \(\sqrt3\) and the same base area \(8\sqrt3\); thus
  \[
  \operatorname{Vol}(C\,D\,M\,N)=8 .
  \]

Adding the two tetrahedral volumes,

\[
\operatorname{Vol}(QPCDMN)=8+8=16 .
\]

---

### 5.  Result  

The volume of the solid \(QPCDMN\) is  

\[
\boxed{16}.
\]<|im_end|>

